1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,864
I changed one prompt.

2
00:00:01,864 --> 00:00:03,500
The overall score went up,

3
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:05,574
from ninety percent to ninety-two.

4
00:00:05,574 --> 00:00:07,484
Everyone would have shipped it.

5
00:00:07,484 --> 00:00:09,994
But one group of customers got much worse.

6
00:00:09,994 --> 00:00:13,377
Refund tickets fell from a hundred percent right to fifty.

7
00:00:13,727 --> 00:00:15,336
A simple automatic check,

8
00:00:15,336 --> 00:00:16,279
an eval gate,

9
00:00:16,279 --> 00:00:18,333
caught it and blocked the change.

10
00:00:18,333 --> 00:00:21,274
This lesson is about shipping A I changes safely,

11
00:00:21,274 --> 00:00:22,384
again and again,

12
00:00:22,384 --> 00:00:24,604
without breaking what already works.

13
00:00:25,854 --> 00:00:29,359
Welcome to lesson seven of the cloud for A I engineers course,

14
00:00:29,359 --> 00:00:30,686
on The Code Notebook.

15
00:00:31,036 --> 00:00:34,171
The handwritten notes and the code for this lesson are linked in

16
00:00:34,171 --> 00:00:35,093
the description.

17
00:00:36,343 --> 00:00:38,954
So far we built the pieces of an A I system.

18
00:00:38,954 --> 00:00:40,641
Now we run it as a product.

19
00:00:40,641 --> 00:00:42,436
That means changing it often,

20
00:00:42,436 --> 00:00:43,742
without breaking it,

21
00:00:43,742 --> 00:00:46,463
and knowing quickly when something goes wrong.

22
00:00:46,813 --> 00:00:48,988
That discipline has two names.

23
00:00:48,988 --> 00:00:50,971
M L Ops for classic models,

24
00:00:50,971 --> 00:00:53,659
and L L M Ops for language model apps.

25
00:00:53,659 --> 00:00:56,026
As always I label my experiments.

26
00:00:56,026 --> 00:00:59,609
The eval gate uses a toy keyword model as a stand in

27
00:00:59,609 --> 00:01:01,209
for a language model,

28
00:01:01,209 --> 00:01:02,936
with synthetic tickets.

29
00:01:02,936 --> 00:01:06,136
The canary and trials results are simulations.

30
00:01:07,386 --> 00:01:09,678
Classic M L Ops has three levels,

31
00:01:09,678 --> 00:01:11,847
from a well known Google guide.

32
00:01:11,847 --> 00:01:14,139
Level zero is everything by hand.

33
00:01:14,139 --> 00:01:17,237
Notebooks, a model handed over to an engineer,

34
00:01:17,237 --> 00:01:19,406
and nobody watching afterwards.

35
00:01:19,756 --> 00:01:21,995
Level one automates the training.

36
00:01:21,995 --> 00:01:25,505
A pipeline retrains the model on fresh data by itself,

37
00:01:25,505 --> 00:01:27,078
when new data arrives,

38
00:01:27,078 --> 00:01:28,168
on a schedule,

39
00:01:28,168 --> 00:01:29,983
or when performance drops.

40
00:01:30,333 --> 00:01:33,558
Level two automates the shipping of that pipeline.

41
00:01:33,558 --> 00:01:38,038
The pipeline code itself is tested and deployed like any other service.

42
00:01:39,288 --> 00:01:41,648
Language model apps change the picture.

43
00:01:41,648 --> 00:01:43,185
Often you train nothing.

44
00:01:43,185 --> 00:01:44,722
You call a hosted model.

45
00:01:44,722 --> 00:01:46,203
So what do you version?

46
00:01:46,203 --> 00:01:47,082
The prompts.

47
00:01:47,082 --> 00:01:48,783
The model and its settings.

48
00:01:48,783 --> 00:01:50,594
The tools the model may call.

49
00:01:50,594 --> 00:01:51,912
The retrieval index.

50
00:01:51,912 --> 00:01:53,833
And the code that wraps it all.

51
00:01:54,183 --> 00:01:56,845
Quality is no longer one accuracy number.

52
00:01:56,845 --> 00:01:58,679
It is a set of evaluations,

53
00:01:58,679 --> 00:02:00,572
plus checks on live traffic.

54
00:02:00,572 --> 00:02:03,649
The thing you watch is the trace of one request.

55
00:02:03,649 --> 00:02:06,607
And the same input can give different outputs,

56
00:02:06,607 --> 00:02:08,678
so you must run several trials.

57
00:02:09,928 --> 00:02:12,207
There is one more reason for all this.

58
00:02:12,207 --> 00:02:14,703
Models decay without anyone touching them.

59
00:02:14,703 --> 00:02:15,896
The world changes,

60
00:02:15,896 --> 00:02:17,633
customers write differently,

61
00:02:17,633 --> 00:02:18,935
new products appear,

62
00:02:18,935 --> 00:02:22,570
and the model's inputs drift away from what it saw in training.

63
00:02:22,920 --> 00:02:24,270
Without monitoring,

64
00:02:24,270 --> 00:02:25,444
nothing crashes.

65
00:02:25,444 --> 00:02:27,675
The answers just get slowly worse.

66
00:02:27,675 --> 00:02:29,964
That is why level zero is so risky.

67
00:02:29,964 --> 00:02:34,072
Classic M L Ops watches the distribution of inputs and the quality

68
00:02:34,072 --> 00:02:34,953
of outputs,

69
00:02:34,953 --> 00:02:37,301
and retrains when they move too far.

70
00:02:38,551 --> 00:02:40,203
Here is the loop to build.

71
00:02:40,203 --> 00:02:41,194
Make a change,

72
00:02:41,194 --> 00:02:42,681
to a prompt or a model.

73
00:02:42,681 --> 00:02:45,269
Score the whole test set with an eval gate.

74
00:02:45,269 --> 00:02:48,078
Send a little traffic to the new version first,

75
00:02:48,078 --> 00:02:49,180
called a canary.

76
00:02:49,530 --> 00:02:53,436
Then watch it with traces and automatic judges on live requests.

77
00:02:53,436 --> 00:02:55,676
When something fails in production,

78
00:02:55,676 --> 00:02:58,146
turn that failure into a new test case.

79
00:02:58,146 --> 00:03:02,282
Every change is then scored against everything that ever went wrong.

80
00:03:02,632 --> 00:03:04,628
That last arrow is the whole point.

81
00:03:04,628 --> 00:03:06,880
The loop gets harder to fool every week.

82
00:03:08,130 --> 00:03:10,476
Testing an A I system has three layers,

83
00:03:10,476 --> 00:03:12,604
and each catches a different break.

84
00:03:12,604 --> 00:03:13,423
Code tests,

85
00:03:13,423 --> 00:03:14,514
the normal kind.

86
00:03:14,514 --> 00:03:17,570
Do the functions work and do the parts fit together?

87
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:19,323
Data and model tests.

88
00:03:19,323 --> 00:03:20,840
Does training converge,

89
00:03:20,840 --> 00:03:22,468
are there missing values,

90
00:03:22,468 --> 00:03:25,444
does the data still look like the reference data?

91
00:03:25,444 --> 00:03:28,027
And evaluation suites for language models,

92
00:03:28,027 --> 00:03:30,442
where graders score answers on a fixed,

93
00:03:30,442 --> 00:03:31,958
golden set of examples.

94
00:03:32,308 --> 00:03:34,109
Run all three on every change.

95
00:03:34,109 --> 00:03:36,439
A failure in any layer blocks the merge.

96
00:03:37,689 --> 00:03:41,207
An eval gate is the check that stands between a change and

97
00:03:41,207 --> 00:03:43,590
production. It asks several questions.

98
00:03:43,590 --> 00:03:47,392
Does the challenger beat the current champion by a real margin,

99
00:03:47,392 --> 00:03:48,640
not just match it?

100
00:03:48,990 --> 00:03:51,196
Does any important slice get worse,

101
00:03:51,196 --> 00:03:53,742
meaning a group of cases such as refunds?

102
00:03:53,742 --> 00:03:55,891
Are the speed and size limits met?

103
00:03:55,891 --> 00:03:59,681
And does the model actually load and serve in a test container?

104
00:03:59,681 --> 00:04:02,849
Only if every answer is yes does the change move on.

105
00:04:04,099 --> 00:04:05,652
I built a working gate.

106
00:04:05,652 --> 00:04:08,818
The task is to route a support ticket to an intent,

107
00:04:08,818 --> 00:04:10,026
refund, delivery,

108
00:04:10,026 --> 00:04:11,350
account or product.

109
00:04:11,350 --> 00:04:12,674
The model is a toy.

110
00:04:12,674 --> 00:04:13,882
A keyword scorer.

111
00:04:13,882 --> 00:04:16,299
It is a stand in for a language model,

112
00:04:16,299 --> 00:04:18,774
and the prompt is its list of keywords.

113
00:04:19,124 --> 00:04:22,604
I generated a hundred synthetic tickets from templates.

114
00:04:22,604 --> 00:04:24,315
Sixty are about delivery,

115
00:04:24,315 --> 00:04:26,556
the rest split between the others,

116
00:04:26,556 --> 00:04:29,800
which is my assumption of a typical imbalanced mix.

117
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:31,687
Version one is the baseline.

118
00:04:31,687 --> 00:04:33,633
Version two adds one keyword,

119
00:04:33,633 --> 00:04:34,754
the word order,

120
00:04:34,754 --> 00:04:35,698
to delivery.

121
00:04:36,948 --> 00:04:38,601
Delivery tickets improved,

122
00:04:38,601 --> 00:04:41,521
from eighty-six point seven percent to a hundred.

123
00:04:41,521 --> 00:04:44,055
Great, the new keyword fixed tickets like,

124
00:04:44,055 --> 00:04:45,267
where is my order.

125
00:04:45,617 --> 00:04:48,649
But the word order also appears in refund tickets.

126
00:04:48,649 --> 00:04:51,343
Refund went from a hundred percent to fifty.

127
00:04:51,343 --> 00:04:53,870
Overall, the score went up by two points,

128
00:04:53,870 --> 00:04:56,509
because delivery tickets were the majority.

129
00:04:56,859 --> 00:04:58,097
The average improved.

130
00:04:58,097 --> 00:05:00,523
The product got worse for an important group.

131
00:05:01,773 --> 00:05:03,322
The gate has two rules.

132
00:05:03,322 --> 00:05:05,387
The overall score must not fall,

133
00:05:05,387 --> 00:05:08,772
and no single intent may drop by more than five points.

134
00:05:08,772 --> 00:05:11,927
Version two passed the first and failed the second.

135
00:05:12,277 --> 00:05:15,346
The gate printed blocked and exited with code one.

136
00:05:15,346 --> 00:05:16,369
In a pipeline,

137
00:05:16,369 --> 00:05:18,528
that failure code stops the merge.

138
00:05:18,528 --> 00:05:20,631
No person had to notice anything.

139
00:05:21,881 --> 00:05:23,333
The lesson is simple.

140
00:05:23,333 --> 00:05:24,786
Averages hide damage.

141
00:05:24,786 --> 00:05:26,296
Always score by slice,

142
00:05:26,296 --> 00:05:28,097
meaning by type of request,

143
00:05:28,097 --> 00:05:29,781
language, customer group,

144
00:05:29,781 --> 00:05:31,001
whatever matters.

145
00:05:31,001 --> 00:05:34,428
A model that is better on average can be much worse for

146
00:05:34,428 --> 00:05:35,299
a minority.

147
00:05:35,649 --> 00:05:37,470
Also keep a regression suite,

148
00:05:37,470 --> 00:05:40,504
a set of cases that must stay at a hundred percent.

149
00:05:40,504 --> 00:05:42,435
New capability tests start low,

150
00:05:42,435 --> 00:05:43,814
like a hill to climb.

151
00:05:43,814 --> 00:05:45,413
When one reaches the top,

152
00:05:45,413 --> 00:05:47,730
graduate it into the regression suite.

153
00:05:48,980 --> 00:05:50,543
How do you choose slices?

154
00:05:50,543 --> 00:05:52,699
Start from your own failure reports.

155
00:05:52,699 --> 00:05:54,693
Group tickets by type of request,

156
00:05:54,693 --> 00:05:55,555
by language,

157
00:05:55,555 --> 00:05:56,687
by customer tier,

158
00:05:56,687 --> 00:05:57,441
by length,

159
00:05:57,441 --> 00:05:59,597
and by which tool the answer needed.

160
00:05:59,597 --> 00:06:02,722
Any group you would be embarrassed to fail is a slice.

161
00:06:03,072 --> 00:06:06,308
Give each slice enough test cases to be meaningful.

162
00:06:06,308 --> 00:06:10,133
Ten refund tickets can move fifty points on a single mistake,

163
00:06:10,133 --> 00:06:14,075
so keep growing the small slices from real production failures.

164
00:06:15,325 --> 00:06:16,697
Who does the grading?

165
00:06:16,697 --> 00:06:18,344
Start with error analysis.

166
00:06:18,344 --> 00:06:20,430
Read real failures and group them.

167
00:06:20,430 --> 00:06:22,187
That tells you what to test.

168
00:06:22,187 --> 00:06:24,163
Then prefer simple code graders,

169
00:06:24,163 --> 00:06:26,195
a program that checks the answer,

170
00:06:26,195 --> 00:06:27,347
wherever you can.

171
00:06:27,697 --> 00:06:31,565
Use a language model as judge only for what code cannot check.

172
00:06:31,565 --> 00:06:33,850
Keep judges to yes or no questions,

173
00:06:33,850 --> 00:06:36,193
and check them against human labels,

174
00:06:36,193 --> 00:06:39,006
so you know the judge itself is trustworthy.

175
00:06:40,256 --> 00:06:41,336
One more trap.

176
00:06:41,336 --> 00:06:44,576
A language model can answer differently each time.

177
00:06:44,576 --> 00:06:47,516
So an eval that runs each case once is noisy.

178
00:06:47,516 --> 00:06:49,676
You run each case several times.

179
00:06:50,026 --> 00:06:51,912
There are two ways to count.

180
00:06:51,912 --> 00:06:55,390
Pass at k asks whether at least one of k runs succeeds.

181
00:06:55,390 --> 00:06:58,337
Pass hat k asks whether all of k runs succeed.

182
00:06:58,337 --> 00:07:00,754
The first measures can it ever do it.

183
00:07:00,754 --> 00:07:03,171
The second measures can I rely on it.

184
00:07:04,421 --> 00:07:05,563
I simulated it.

185
00:07:05,563 --> 00:07:08,990
Suppose an agent succeeds ninety percent of the time.

186
00:07:08,990 --> 00:07:10,313
That sounds great.

187
00:07:10,313 --> 00:07:11,636
Run it five times,

188
00:07:11,636 --> 00:07:15,724
and the chance that all five succeed is only fifty-nine percent.

189
00:07:16,074 --> 00:07:16,833
Ten runs,

190
00:07:16,833 --> 00:07:18,236
thirty-five percent.

191
00:07:18,236 --> 00:07:20,397
And with an eighty percent agent,

192
00:07:20,397 --> 00:07:23,728
all ten runs succeed just eleven percent of the time.

193
00:07:23,728 --> 00:07:26,065
Reliability falls fast with repeats.

194
00:07:26,065 --> 00:07:30,213
I checked both formulas against a hundred thousand simulated tasks,

195
00:07:30,213 --> 00:07:31,440
and they matched.

196
00:07:32,690 --> 00:07:34,962
Here is the gate as a pipeline file,

197
00:07:34,962 --> 00:07:36,325
a sketch for GitHub.

198
00:07:36,325 --> 00:07:37,801
On every pull request,

199
00:07:37,801 --> 00:07:39,107
run the unit tests,

200
00:07:39,107 --> 00:07:40,243
the data checks,

201
00:07:40,243 --> 00:07:41,947
and the evaluation suites.

202
00:07:41,947 --> 00:07:45,127
Any step that exits with a failure blocks the merge.

203
00:07:45,477 --> 00:07:47,987
The same idea works in any C I system.

204
00:07:47,987 --> 00:07:50,796
What matters is that the gate is automatic,

205
00:07:50,796 --> 00:07:52,290
runs on every change,

206
00:07:52,290 --> 00:07:55,158
and cannot be skipped by someone in a hurry.

207
00:07:56,408 --> 00:07:58,446
A registry answers one question.

208
00:07:58,446 --> 00:08:00,766
Which exact version is in production,

209
00:08:00,766 --> 00:08:01,898
who approved it,

210
00:08:01,898 --> 00:08:03,369
and what did it score?

211
00:08:03,369 --> 00:08:05,916
Today a registry tracks more than models.

212
00:08:05,916 --> 00:08:07,048
Prompts, judges,

213
00:08:07,048 --> 00:08:08,066
test datasets,

214
00:08:08,066 --> 00:08:09,311
even tool servers.

215
00:08:09,661 --> 00:08:10,614
Use aliases,

216
00:08:10,614 --> 00:08:11,507
not stages.

217
00:08:11,507 --> 00:08:14,723
Instead of moving a model into a production stage,

218
00:08:14,723 --> 00:08:18,296
you point a name like champion at one immutable version.

219
00:08:18,296 --> 00:08:21,511
Promoting or rolling back is just moving the name.

220
00:08:22,761 --> 00:08:24,322
Treat prompts like code.

221
00:08:24,322 --> 00:08:25,827
A prompt is a template,

222
00:08:25,827 --> 00:08:26,831
its variables,

223
00:08:26,831 --> 00:08:28,336
and the model settings,

224
00:08:28,336 --> 00:08:30,287
stored outside the application.

225
00:08:30,287 --> 00:08:32,739
Every change is a new immutable version.

226
00:08:32,739 --> 00:08:34,523
You never edit one in place.

227
00:08:34,873 --> 00:08:38,066
The app fetches the prompt by alias and caches it.

228
00:08:38,066 --> 00:08:40,845
So moving the alias is an instant rollback,

229
00:08:40,845 --> 00:08:41,791
no redeploy.

230
00:08:41,791 --> 00:08:44,569
And every trace records the prompt version,

231
00:08:44,569 --> 00:08:46,993
so you can slice failures by version.

232
00:08:48,243 --> 00:08:50,459
A model card is the label on a model.

233
00:08:50,459 --> 00:08:52,081
Short settings at the top,

234
00:08:52,081 --> 00:08:53,107
in a few lines.

235
00:08:53,107 --> 00:08:54,188
What base model,

236
00:08:54,188 --> 00:08:54,945
what data,

237
00:08:54,945 --> 00:08:55,864
what licence,

238
00:08:55,864 --> 00:08:57,323
what kind of fine tune.

239
00:08:57,323 --> 00:08:58,458
Then prose below.

240
00:08:58,458 --> 00:08:59,485
What it is for,

241
00:08:59,485 --> 00:09:00,296
its limits,

242
00:09:00,296 --> 00:09:01,539
how it was trained,

243
00:09:01,539 --> 00:09:02,511
how it scored.

244
00:09:02,861 --> 00:09:06,273
On Hugging Face the model card is simply the readme file.

245
00:09:06,273 --> 00:09:09,797
Writing one forces you to say what your model should not be

246
00:09:09,797 --> 00:09:10,524
used for.

247
00:09:11,774 --> 00:09:13,045
Now the machines.

248
00:09:13,045 --> 00:09:17,280
Infrastructure as code means describing your cloud setup in files.

249
00:09:17,280 --> 00:09:20,850
Tools called Terraform and OpenTofu create the network,

250
00:09:20,850 --> 00:09:21,818
the cluster,

251
00:09:21,818 --> 00:09:23,391
the GPU machine pools,

252
00:09:23,391 --> 00:09:24,601
the permissions,

253
00:09:24,601 --> 00:09:25,811
and the storage.

254
00:09:26,161 --> 00:09:27,328
A second layer,

255
00:09:27,328 --> 00:09:28,434
called GitOps,

256
00:09:28,434 --> 00:09:31,505
uses a tool like Argo C D or Flux to keep what

257
00:09:31,505 --> 00:09:34,454
runs in the cluster matching what is in Git.

258
00:09:34,454 --> 00:09:35,560
A third layer,

259
00:09:35,560 --> 00:09:39,184
rollouts, does canary releases with automatic rollback.

260
00:09:40,434 --> 00:09:41,522
Why this style?

261
00:09:41,522 --> 00:09:44,727
Git becomes the source of truth and the audit trail.

262
00:09:44,727 --> 00:09:47,188
If someone changes the cluster by hand,

263
00:09:47,188 --> 00:09:49,363
the tool notices and puts it back.

264
00:09:49,363 --> 00:09:51,995
And a rollback is just reverting a commit.

265
00:09:52,345 --> 00:09:54,290
One more rule from the notebook.

266
00:09:54,290 --> 00:09:57,368
Keep your model provider keys out of the state files,

267
00:09:57,368 --> 00:10:00,230
using the tools' ephemeral and write only values.

268
00:10:00,230 --> 00:10:02,823
A leaked state file should never leak a key.

269
00:10:04,073 --> 00:10:05,997
Here is a taste of the code,

270
00:10:05,997 --> 00:10:07,321
from the notebook.

271
00:10:07,321 --> 00:10:10,087
A pool of spot GPU machines for inference.

272
00:10:10,087 --> 00:10:11,531
The minimum is zero,

273
00:10:11,531 --> 00:10:14,177
so with no traffic there is no GPU bill.

274
00:10:14,177 --> 00:10:15,620
The maximum is four.

275
00:10:15,620 --> 00:10:17,184
The machines are spot,

276
00:10:17,184 --> 00:10:19,169
so cheaper and interruptible.

277
00:10:19,519 --> 00:10:22,115
Notice that it ties back to lesson two.

278
00:10:22,115 --> 00:10:23,202
Scale to zero,

279
00:10:23,202 --> 00:10:25,858
spot, and a GPU taint from lesson three,

280
00:10:25,858 --> 00:10:27,368
all in a dozen lines.

281
00:10:27,368 --> 00:10:32,017
Check the current provider documentation before using anything like this,

282
00:10:32,017 --> 00:10:33,707
because settings change.

283
00:10:34,957 --> 00:10:36,068
Now the canary.

284
00:10:36,068 --> 00:10:37,587
Even with a good gate,

285
00:10:37,587 --> 00:10:39,690
real traffic finds new problems.

286
00:10:39,690 --> 00:10:43,021
So you send the new version to a small share of users

287
00:10:43,021 --> 00:10:47,403
first, and an automatic check decides whether to continue or roll back.

288
00:10:47,753 --> 00:10:48,870
I simulated it.

289
00:10:48,870 --> 00:10:50,870
Six hundred requests a minute.

290
00:10:50,870 --> 00:10:53,106
The old version fails one percent.

291
00:10:53,106 --> 00:10:56,047
The new version is bad and fails five percent.

292
00:10:56,047 --> 00:10:58,047
Both rates are my assumptions.

293
00:10:58,047 --> 00:11:00,811
An automatic check runs every five minutes,

294
00:11:00,811 --> 00:11:04,988
and rolls back if failures are far above what one percent predicts.

295
00:11:06,238 --> 00:11:08,049
Release to everyone at once,

296
00:11:08,049 --> 00:11:11,842
and about a hundred and fifty bad responses went out before the

297
00:11:11,842 --> 00:11:14,276
rollback. A twenty-five percent canary,

298
00:11:14,276 --> 00:11:16,540
thirty-eight. A five percent canary,

299
00:11:16,540 --> 00:11:17,672
nine point four.

300
00:11:17,672 --> 00:11:19,087
A one percent canary,

301
00:11:19,087 --> 00:11:19,596
four.

302
00:11:19,946 --> 00:11:23,465
The rule of thumb is that the damage scales with the share

303
00:11:23,465 --> 00:11:24,941
of traffic you expose.

304
00:11:24,941 --> 00:11:27,892
A small canary turns a disaster into a footnote.

305
00:11:29,142 --> 00:11:30,590
But small is not free.

306
00:11:30,590 --> 00:11:32,262
With a one percent canary,

307
00:11:32,262 --> 00:11:36,385
the very first check caught the problem only forty-five percent of the

308
00:11:36,385 --> 00:11:38,892
time. There were not enough requests yet.

309
00:11:38,892 --> 00:11:41,789
It took about two point seven checks on average.

310
00:11:42,139 --> 00:11:46,477
A five percent canary caught it first time seventy-seven percent of the

311
00:11:46,477 --> 00:11:50,063
time. Bigger canaries decide faster and expose more users.

312
00:11:50,063 --> 00:11:53,418
Pick a share small enough to be safe and big enough to

313
00:11:53,418 --> 00:11:54,459
learn quickly.

314
00:11:55,709 --> 00:11:56,723
After release,

315
00:11:56,723 --> 00:11:57,512
you watch.

316
00:11:57,512 --> 00:12:00,835
For language model apps the unit to watch is the trace,

317
00:12:00,835 --> 00:12:02,243
one tree per request,

318
00:12:02,243 --> 00:12:03,821
showing each model call,

319
00:12:03,821 --> 00:12:05,961
each tool call and each retrieval,

320
00:12:05,961 --> 00:12:07,257
with time and cost.

321
00:12:07,607 --> 00:12:10,034
The tooling market is changing fast.

322
00:12:10,034 --> 00:12:12,339
Langfuse was bought by ClickHouse,

323
00:12:12,339 --> 00:12:13,796
Promptfoo by OpenAI,

324
00:12:13,796 --> 00:12:16,465
and Helicone went into maintenance mode,

325
00:12:16,465 --> 00:12:18,285
according to the notebook.

326
00:12:18,285 --> 00:12:22,168
So record traces in the open O Tel standard for generative A

327
00:12:22,168 --> 00:12:24,595
I, and your backend stays swappable.

328
00:12:25,845 --> 00:12:28,302
Graders can run on live traffic too.

329
00:12:28,302 --> 00:12:31,741
An online judge samples real traces and scores them,

330
00:12:31,741 --> 00:12:32,724
for example,

331
00:12:32,724 --> 00:12:34,751
did the answer cite a source,

332
00:12:34,751 --> 00:12:37,085
or did the user have to ask again.

333
00:12:37,085 --> 00:12:39,419
Scores feed dashboards and alerts.

334
00:12:39,769 --> 00:12:40,982
When a trace fails,

335
00:12:40,982 --> 00:12:42,618
save it as a new test case.

336
00:12:42,618 --> 00:12:44,622
That closes the loop from earlier.

337
00:12:44,622 --> 00:12:46,522
Production teaches the test set.

338
00:12:47,772 --> 00:12:50,733
For language model apps that train nothing,

339
00:12:50,733 --> 00:12:54,892
the idea of continuous training becomes continuous evaluation.

340
00:12:54,892 --> 00:12:57,538
Judges score live traces all the time.

341
00:12:57,538 --> 00:12:59,932
Prompt optimisers propose changes.

342
00:12:59,932 --> 00:13:04,091
Every candidate passes through the gate before an alias moves.

343
00:13:04,441 --> 00:13:08,389
A registry hook or an alias change can start the deployment job,

344
00:13:08,389 --> 00:13:11,466
which runs a canary with an alarm based rollback.

345
00:13:11,466 --> 00:13:13,381
People review the dashboards,

346
00:13:13,381 --> 00:13:14,543
not each change.

347
00:13:15,793 --> 00:13:17,452
One more thing to watch.

348
00:13:17,452 --> 00:13:19,468
Cost. For language model apps,

349
00:13:19,468 --> 00:13:23,143
the cost per token and per request deserves its own alarm,

350
00:13:23,143 --> 00:13:24,210
next to speed.

351
00:13:24,210 --> 00:13:28,299
A prompt change that doubles the input length can double the bill

352
00:13:28,299 --> 00:13:29,129
overnight.

353
00:13:29,479 --> 00:13:31,318
Record the cost on every trace,

354
00:13:31,318 --> 00:13:33,262
and add a cost check to the gate.

355
00:13:33,262 --> 00:13:35,153
Lesson eleven goes deep on this.

356
00:13:36,403 --> 00:13:37,892
For ShopNest Copilot.

357
00:13:37,892 --> 00:13:39,738
Prompts live in a registry,

358
00:13:39,738 --> 00:13:42,180
with versions and a production alias.

359
00:13:42,180 --> 00:13:44,742
Every pull request runs the unit tests,

360
00:13:44,742 --> 00:13:47,362
a golden set of tickets scored by slice,

361
00:13:47,362 --> 00:13:48,613
and a cost check.

362
00:13:48,963 --> 00:13:50,583
Infrastructure is code.

363
00:13:50,583 --> 00:13:53,763
New versions go to five percent of traffic first,

364
00:13:53,763 --> 00:13:55,623
with an automatic rollback.

365
00:13:55,623 --> 00:13:58,023
Traces and online judges watch live,

366
00:13:58,023 --> 00:14:00,783
and failed traces become new golden cases.

367
00:14:02,033 --> 00:14:03,844
Four words to carry forward.

368
00:14:03,844 --> 00:14:04,637
Eval gate:

369
00:14:04,637 --> 00:14:07,410
an automatic test that blocks a worse change.

370
00:14:07,410 --> 00:14:08,994
Champion and challenger:

371
00:14:08,994 --> 00:14:11,541
the current version and the proposed one.

372
00:14:11,891 --> 00:14:14,236
Canary: sending a little traffic first.

373
00:14:14,236 --> 00:14:14,999
And trace:

374
00:14:14,999 --> 00:14:17,453
the record of everything one request did.

375
00:14:18,703 --> 00:14:19,915
Interview corner.

376
00:14:19,915 --> 00:14:22,570
Asked how you ship a prompt change safely,

377
00:14:22,570 --> 00:14:23,724
say: version it,

378
00:14:23,724 --> 00:14:27,591
run an eval gate that checks the overall score and every slice,

379
00:14:27,591 --> 00:14:31,400
canary it on a small share of traffic with automatic rollback,

380
00:14:31,400 --> 00:14:32,613
and watch traces.

381
00:14:32,613 --> 00:14:33,709
In my toy gate,

382
00:14:33,709 --> 00:14:37,518
a change that raised the average by two points was blocked for

383
00:14:37,518 --> 00:14:39,250
dropping refunds by fifty.

384
00:14:39,600 --> 00:14:42,359
Asked why run evaluations several times,

385
00:14:42,359 --> 00:14:44,178
say: model output varies,

386
00:14:44,178 --> 00:14:47,126
and reliability falls quickly with repeats.

387
00:14:47,126 --> 00:14:51,390
A ninety percent agent passes five runs in a row only fifty-nine

388
00:14:51,390 --> 00:14:52,895
percent of the time.

389
00:14:54,145 --> 00:14:54,917
Your turn.

390
00:14:54,917 --> 00:14:57,453
The zip for this lesson has three scripts.

391
00:14:57,453 --> 00:14:58,445
The eval gate,

392
00:14:58,445 --> 00:14:59,878
the trials calculator,

393
00:14:59,878 --> 00:15:01,477
and the canary simulator.

394
00:15:01,477 --> 00:15:04,068
Change the keyword list so the gate passes,

395
00:15:04,068 --> 00:15:05,777
or change the canary share.

396
00:15:06,127 --> 00:15:09,877
Try adding a keyword to refund tickets that fixes the problem,

397
00:15:09,877 --> 00:15:11,696
and watch the gate go green.

398
00:15:11,696 --> 00:15:15,105
One changed number teaches more than an hour of reading.

399
00:15:16,355 --> 00:15:18,094
Recap. Ship the pipeline,

400
00:15:18,094 --> 00:15:19,473
not just the model.

401
00:15:19,473 --> 00:15:21,332
Run a gate on every change,

402
00:15:21,332 --> 00:15:22,112
by slice,

403
00:15:22,112 --> 00:15:24,091
because averages hide damage.

404
00:15:24,091 --> 00:15:27,989
My toy gate blocked a plus two point change that cost refunds

405
00:15:27,989 --> 00:15:29,008
fifty points.

406
00:15:29,008 --> 00:15:30,387
Run several trials,

407
00:15:30,387 --> 00:15:34,945
since a ninety percent agent passes five runs only fifty-nine percent of

408
00:15:34,945 --> 00:15:35,724
the time.

409
00:15:36,074 --> 00:15:37,151
Move aliases,

410
00:15:37,151 --> 00:15:37,975
not code,

411
00:15:37,975 --> 00:15:39,812
to promote and roll back.

412
00:15:39,812 --> 00:15:40,889
Canary small,

413
00:15:40,889 --> 00:15:45,007
and the damage in my simulation fell from about a hundred and

414
00:15:45,007 --> 00:15:47,034
fifty bad responses to nine.

415
00:15:47,034 --> 00:15:48,048
Next lesson:

416
00:15:48,048 --> 00:15:50,012
G E N A I app architecture.

417
00:15:50,012 --> 00:15:50,645
R A G,

418
00:15:50,645 --> 00:15:51,976
agents and tools.

419
00:15:53,226 --> 00:15:54,502
In the next lesson:

420
00:15:54,502 --> 00:15:55,833
generative A I apps.

421
00:15:55,833 --> 00:15:57,054
Starting with rag,

422
00:15:57,054 --> 00:15:59,051
from documents to cited answers.

423
00:15:59,401 --> 00:16:00,993
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

424
00:16:00,993 --> 00:16:02,267
See you in the notebook.

